# Apply a `context/` delta to an existing candidate (incremental, vN.M → vN.M+1)

**Inputs** (the orchestrator, `prompts/build-incremental.prompt`, passes
the first three; invoked standalone, the runner resolves them the same
way it does):
- The base candidate `../output/v<N.M>-sgai-spec.md` — the most recent
  candidate in `../output/`.
- `ANCHOR` — the commit whose `context/` the base candidate was built
  from, as printed by `bin/check-incremental.py anchor v<N.M>`. Never
  work it out by hand: the script is the definition.
- `HEAD` — `git rev-parse HEAD`. `context/` MUST have no uncommitted
  changes (`git status --porcelain -- context/` prints nothing);
  otherwise the delta applied is not the delta recorded, and the step
  refuses to start.
- The delta itself:
  `git log --format='%h %s' ANCHOR..HEAD -- context/` and
  `git diff ANCHOR..HEAD -- context/`.
- `../context/` at `HEAD`, read-only, for the full text around each
  changed hunk.
- `../context-analysis/` as regenerated by Stage 1 of the incremental
  path: `dash-gap-analysis.md` (net-new vs extension of DASH 6th
  machinery), `error-semantics.md` (chapter 8 and the test-cases
  annex), `iab-ad-templates.md` (chapter 3 vocabulary).
- The primary copy of the base standard declared in
  `../context/00-normative-base.md`, for grounding the new or changed
  normative content only.

**Output**:
- `../output/v<N.M+1>-sgai-spec.md` — the base candidate with the delta
  applied. Only the sections the delta affects change; every other
  section is byte-identical to the base.
- `../output-analysis/v<N.M+1>-context-delta.md` — the trace: every
  change in `context/` between `ANCHOR` and `HEAD`, mapped to where it
  landed in the spec, or to why it did not. Format in §5; it is parsed
  by `bin/check-incremental.py`, so the format is a contract.

`v<N.M+1>` follows the versioning rule of
`prompts/4-auto-refine/refine-spec.prompt` (`v9` → `v9.1`,
`v9.1` → `v9.2`).

**Skip if**: both outputs exist AND the trace records `Context base:`
equal to `ANCHOR` and `Context head:` equal to `HEAD` AND both are newer
than this prompt. The comparison is by commit, not by mtime: the delta
is defined by two commits, and so is whether it was already applied.

**Execution mode**: fresh subagent context. The agent MAY open the
primary copy of the base standard, and only for content the delta
introduces or changes; the rest of the spec was grounded when it was
built and is not re-grounded here. Log `[GROUNDED_BY=iso-23009-1-2026-pdf]`
as the first line if it does, `[GROUNDED_BY=spec-only]` if nothing in
the delta needed it.

---

## What this prompt is for

The pipeline has three iteration scales. A **major** (`build-all`)
regenerates the spec from `context/`. A **minor** (`refine-spec`)
fixes what the analyses found and by contract takes no new
requirement. This prompt is the one in between: `context/` moved by a
bounded amount, and the spec has to absorb exactly that movement and
nothing else.

The value of the scale depends on one property: **what the delta does
not touch stays as it was.** A spec that has been validated, audited
and refined carries work that a regeneration throws away and has to
buy back. An incremental that rewrites untouched sections throws it
away just the same, and hides it.

## Constraints

- **Edit the base, do not rewrite it.** Copy
  `../output/v<N.M>-sgai-spec.md` to `../output/v<N.M+1>-sgai-spec.md`
  and apply each change as an in-place edit of that copy. Never emit
  the whole document from memory: byte-identity of untouched sections
  is checked afterwards (`bin/check-incremental.py scope`), and a
  regenerated section fails that check even when it reads the same.
- **The same rules as `prompts/2-build/build-spec.prompt`** for every
  byte that is new: self-contained, no R-numbers, no reference to
  `../context-analysis/`, attribute tables in chapter 5, no
  prohibitions of the spec's own (DP-2), RFC 2119 vocabulary. **A
  criterion that says MUST or MUST NOT arrives with that keyword**: restating an obligation in the
  indicative is the `force-lost` defect the validation step exists to
  catch, and an incremental that introduces it has degraded the
  document.
- **One heading, one section.** Section identity is the heading text,
  and it must stay unique. Do not rename an existing heading unless
  the delta requires it; a rename is a removed section plus an added
  one, and both are declared.
- **Every edit is annotated** with `<!-- delta: <commit> <unit> -->`
  on the line above the edited content, `<commit>` the short hash
  that introduced the change and `<unit>` its context identifier
  (`R35.2`, `UC-14`, `99-glossary: base specification`). Existing
  `<!-- refine: ... -->` annotations stay where they are.
- **What the delta retires, the spec retires.** A criterion removed or
  relaxed in `context/` is removed or relaxed in the spec, and
  declared like any other change.
- **Knock-on edits are part of the delta.** A new use case needs its
  annex (with the content `build-spec.prompt` requires of a UC annex)
  and its rows in the test-cases annex; a new or removed heading moves
  the table of contents. Each such section is declared.
- **A `## Refinement gaps` section carried by the base** is kept. A row
  the delta resolves is removed from it, and the section is declared.
- **Never touch** `../context/`, `../context-analysis/`, `../dist/`,
  `../prompts/`, the base candidate, or any existing file in
  `../output-analysis/` other than the trace this step writes.

## Task

### 1. Collect the delta

List every hunk of `git diff ANCHOR..HEAD -- context/`, and name the
unit each hunk belongs to: a criterion (`R<n>.<m>`), a requirement's
prose (`R<n>`), a design principle (`DP-<n>`), an out-of-scope item
(`OOS-<n>`), a use case (`UC-<n>`), a glossary entry, a naming or
carrier convention in `06-naming-and-namespaces.md`, or the file
itself when none of those applies. Attach the commit that introduced
it (`git log -L` or `git blame` over the range, whichever is quicker).
Classify each: `added`, `modified`, `removed`, or `reworded` (same
obligation, different words).

### 2. Place each change

The spec never cites R-numbers, so a unit is found by what it says:
search the base candidate for the construct, the actor and the
obligation the unit concerns. For each change, decide one of:

- **placed** — the spec has, or can take, a home for it with a local
  edit. Apply the edit (§3).
- **no spec effect** — the change cannot alter what the spec states:
  an informative file, a rewording with the same obligation that the
  spec already expresses correctly, a governance note. Say why.
- **not placed**, with one class:
  - `needs-structure` — carrying it would need a new construct, a
    construct retired, a new chapter-level structure, or a change to
    an architectural choice the spec is built around. That is what a
    major is for; do not attempt it here.
  - `ambiguous-source` — `context/` can be read two ways, and the
    readings would put different obligations in the spec. Quote the
    passage and both readings.

### 3. Apply

For each placed change, the minimal edit that carries it, in the
surrounding wording and structure. Ground new normative claims about
DASH 6th against the primary copy: cite the clause **and quote the
sentence**, and tag anything that could not be verified as
`[inferred]`. Record every section whose bytes changed.

### 4. Check before writing the trace

Diff the two files (`diff ../output/v<N.M>-sgai-spec.md
../output/v<N.M+1>-sgai-spec.md`) and confirm that every changed
region sits under a heading you recorded, and that every recorded
heading has a changed region. The orchestrator runs the same check as
a script and rejects the candidate on a mismatch; finding it here is
cheaper.

### 5. Write the trace

`../output-analysis/v<N.M+1>-context-delta.md`, exactly this shape:

```markdown
# v<N.M+1> — context delta applied to v<N.M>

- Base candidate: `output/v<N.M>-sgai-spec.md`
- Context base: `<ANCHOR, full hash>` (from `bin/check-incremental.py anchor v<N.M>`)
- Context head: `<HEAD, full hash>`
- Delta: <c> commits, <h> hunks, <f> files — <p> placed, <e> no spec effect, <u> not placed

## Commits

| Commit | Subject |
|--------|---------|

## Changes placed

| # | Commit | Context unit | Change | Spec section(s) | Edit |
|---|--------|--------------|--------|-----------------|------|

## No spec effect

| # | Commit | Context unit | Change | Why |
|---|--------|--------------|--------|-----|

## Not placed

| # | Commit | Context unit | Change | Class | Why |
|---|--------|--------------|--------|-------|-----|

## Sections changed

| Section | Change | Rows |
|---------|--------|------|
```

- `Context head:` is what the next incremental reads as its anchor, so
  it is the full hash, written once.
- In **Sections changed**, `Section` is the heading text exactly as it
  appears in the spec, without the leading `#`s (`4.5 Player
  conformance`); `Change` is `modified`, `added` or `removed`; `Rows`
  lists the `#` of the placed changes that edited it. One row per
  section, including knock-on sections (table of contents, test-cases
  annex, Refinement gaps).
- `Class` in **Not placed** is `needs-structure` or `ambiguous-source`,
  nothing else. A `needs-structure` row makes the orchestrator
  recommend a major, which is the intended effect.
- **Every hunk lands in exactly one of the three change tables**, and
  `p + e + u` equals the number of changes collected in §1. A hunk in
  none of them is a requirement that silently did not arrive.

## Reporting (stdout)

```
wrote ../output/v<N.M+1>-sgai-spec.md, <NN> lines, <S> sections changed; trace ../output-analysis/v<N.M+1>-context-delta.md: <p> placed, <e> no spec effect, <u> not placed (<k> needs-structure)
```

## What this prompt explicitly does NOT do

- Does NOT regenerate, re-order or re-word sections the delta does not
  reach, even where it would improve them — that is a minor's job,
  driven by the analyses.
- Does NOT fix issues from the base's analysis sidecars.
- Does NOT resolve a `needs-structure` or `ambiguous-source` change by
  choosing for the operator.
- Does NOT run the post-spec analyses; the orchestrator does.

Language: English.
